    "machine": "Apple M1 Pro, 8 cores, 16 GB, Node 25.2.1; everything on this one laptop (no network)",
    "query": "revenue and order count per product (1,000 products), sorted by revenue",
    "systems": {
      "vectro": "live-sync branch: serve({ live }) + connectLive; runs the query again on a change and sends only the difference (see the live section)",
      "hasura": "Hasura GraphQL Engine v2.50.8 + PostgreSQL 16.15, both in Docker; the query is a view (no GROUP BY in Hasura GraphQL), watched as a subscription (graphql-transport-ws); Hasura runs it again every refetch interval (1,000 ms default; also 100 ms) and sends the whole answer when it changed",
      "firestore": "Cloud Firestore emulator v1.22.0 (firebase-tools 15.35.0, JS SDK 13.2.0, Java 21); Firestore can't keep a GROUP BY live, so the writer keeps the per-product totals itself (one batch: the order update + FieldValue.increment on its product's total) and the client listens to the totals ordered by revenue (onSnapshot); writer and listener are separate clients"
    },
    "load": "Vectro 2.31 before / 1.83 after; Hasura 1 s 3.18 / 3.86; Hasura 100 ms 3.64 / 2.91; Firestore 3.85 / 4.61 (one benchmark at a time; the emulator's own Java pushed the load up during its run)",
    "note": "p50 / p99 ms from the write's start to the client receiving (Vectro: and applying) the change. Vectro: 200 samples, bytes = WebSocket text per change. Hasura: 60 samples at 1 s and 100 at 100 ms, random pauses so changes land anywhere in the refetch cycle; bytes = the subscription message (the whole 1,000-row answer). Firestore: 200 samples; bytes not measured (gRPC), docs = changed documents in the snapshot; 1M orders not loaded (the listener reads only the 1,000 totals, so the order count doesn't change its work; loading 1M into the emulator would take about 4 minutes). The emulator is not production Firestore: production adds the network round trip to Google's servers. Postgres query time for the view: 4.9 ms at 10k rows, 75-97 ms at 1M.",
